  "response": "Read all four call sites plus `button.tsx` to ground the proposal.\n\n## The finding\n\n`crime_00064` — `duplicate_component_shape`, medium / confidence 0.70, hash `e96b55b64336`. Four occurrences, no other matches in the repo (`grep \"mt-6 flex justify-end gap-4\"` returns exactly those four):\n\n| File | Lines |\n|---|---|\n| `dashboard/final-example/app/ui/invoices/create-form.tsx` | 142–150 |\n| `dashboard/final-example/app/ui/invoices/edit-form.tsx` | 152–160 |\n| `dashboard/starter-example/app/ui/invoices/create-form.tsx` | 101–109 |\n| `dashboard/starter-example/app/ui/invoices/edit-form.tsx` | 112–120 |\n\nThe subtree is the form footer action bar: a right-aligned flex row holding a `Cancel` `<Link>` and a submit `<Button>`. **The only thing that varies across all four copies is the button label** (`Create Invoice` / `Edit Invoice`). The `<Link>` — href `/dashboard/invoices` and a 10-class `className` string — is byte-identical in all four. That's the drift surface: any change to the cancel link's styling, its href, or the bar's spacing has to be made four times by hand.\n\n## Scoping caveat: this is two extractions, not one\n\n`final-example` and `starter-example` are **separate Next.js apps**, each with its own `@/app/*` alias and its own `app/ui/button.tsx` (that's `crime_00043` — `Button` is already duplicated across the two apps, as are `Breadcrumbs`, `InvoiceStatus`, `skeletons`, and `data.ts`). They share no module graph. So the honest answer is one shared component **per app**, not one across all four sites. Hoisting a 9-line action bar into a new workspace package to unify two copies of a tutorial app is out of proportion — and it wouldn't fix the larger cross-app duplication anyway.\n\nThat means the refactor removes 4 copies → 2, and trades one `duplicate_component_shape` finding for one more cross-app `exact_duplicate_block` pair in the same family as the existing Button/Breadcrumbs/status pairs. Net: real win inside each app, no change to the fixture's inherent two-apps-one-tutorial duplication.\n\n## Files that change\n\n**New (2):**\n- `dashboard/final-example/app/ui/form-actions.tsx`\n- `dashboard/starter-example/app/ui/form-actions.tsx`\n\nSibling of `button.tsx`, not under `invoices/` — `cancelHref` is a prop, so it's a layout primitive, not an invoice concept.\n\n**Edited (4):** the four call sites above. Each drops its 9-line `<div>`, its `import Link from 'next/link'`, and its `import { Button }` (neither is used elsewhere in any of the four files — verified), and gains one `import { FormActions }`.\n\n## Public API\n\n```tsx\n// app/ui/form-actions.tsx\nimport Link from 'next/link';\nimport { Button } from '@/app/ui/button';\n\nexport function FormActions({\n  cancelHref,\n  submitLabel,\n  cancelLabel = 'Cancel',\n  children,\n}: {\n  cancelHref: string;          // required — '/dashboard/invoices' at all 4 sites today\n  submitLabel: string;         // required — the one thing that actually differs\n  cancelLabel?: string;        // default 'Cancel'\n  children?: React.ReactNode;  // slot: extra actions, rendered between cancel and submit\n}) {\n  return (\n    <div className=\"mt-6 flex justify-end gap-4\">\n      <Link\n        href={cancelHref}\n        className=\"flex h-10 items-center rounded-lg bg-gray-100 px-4 text-sm font-medium text-gray-600 transition-colors hover:bg-gray-200\"\n      >\n        {cancelLabel}\n      </Link>\n      {children}\n      <Button type=\"submit\">{submitLabel}</Button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n**Props:** `cancelHref` (required), `submitLabel` (required), `cancelLabel` (optional, defaults to `'Cancel'`).\n\n**Slots:** one — `children`, rendered *between* the cancel link and the submit button, for future secondary actions (Delete, Save draft). The submit button is deliberately **not** a slot: making it one would push `import { Button }` back into every call site and re-open the exact drift path this closes. If a caller later needs a pending/disabled submit state, add a `submitDisabled?: boolean` prop rather than opening the slot.\n\n**No `'use client'` directive.** The component has no hooks or event handlers, so it inherits the caller's boundary — which matters here, because `starter-example/create-form.tsx` is a Server Component while the other three are Client Components. One file serves both.\n\n## Call sites after\n\n```tsx\n// final-example/app/ui/invoices/create-form.tsx\n<FormActions cancelHref=\"/dashboard/invoices\" submitLabel=\"Create Invoice\" />\n\n// final-example/app/ui/invoices/edit-form.tsx\n<FormActions cancelHref=\"/dashboard/invoices\" submitLabel=\"Edit Invoice\" />\n```\n\n…and identically in `starter-example`.\n\n## Deliberately not parameterized\n\n`className` / style overrides on the wrapper. Every caller wants the same bar; an escape hatch would let the spacing drift right back apart, which is the finding.\n\n## Adjacent, out of scope\n\nThe scan flags three more things in these same four files that a component extraction does *not* fix, and shouldn't be bundled into it:\n\n- `crime_00065` / `crime_00066` — `invoice.status === 'pending'` / `'paid'` compared in 4 files across 2 expression shapes. Wants a policy helper, not a component.\n- `crime_00006` — the `\"status\"` literal in 5 production files including `actions.ts`.\n- `crime_00072/73/76/77` — `h-[18px] w-[18px]` and `px-[14px]` design-token escapes, all in the form *body*, above the subtree being extracted.\n\nTo confirm the refactor: re-run `crimes scan --format json` and check that `crime_00064`'s hash `e96b55b64336` no longer appears.",
